  business: "Air One Air Conditioning, Heating, Plumbing & Electric (Colton, California)", type: "trades",
  job: "Answers after-hours and weekend calls in English and Spanish as \"Jenny,\" recognizes returning customers, triages emergencies, and books jobs straight onto the ServiceTitan schedule — including same-night emergency slots once the shop set job types and after-hours tech shifts correctly.",
  story: "Air One is a family business with about 12 employees in Colton, California. After 5 p.m. and on weekends, calls used to roll to a third-party answering service that took messages but could not book into the shop's scheduling software. By morning, CSR/dispatcher Julissa Moulder often heard that the homeowner had already called someone else. The old service was slow to pick up, could not book Spanish-speaking callers, and — by Moulder's recollection — converted somewhere around 30% of after-hours calls in peak summer. The shop already ran on ServiceTitan, so it turned on ServiceTitan's AI Virtual Agent, named Jenny after a favorite coworker. Early on Jenny offered next-day slots for plumbing emergencies; the team rebuilt job types around urgency and gave her access to after-hours technician shifts so urgent calls could book that night.",
  results: ["Between March and mid-June, Air One's internal tracking (after filtering non-leads) showed nearly every after-hours lead converting to a booked call (per ServiceTitan).", "Moulder estimates the old answering service converted around 30% of after-hours calls in summer; ServiceTitan notes those two figures are not the same period or measure.", "A second agent now covers daytime overflow; the shop is still deciding on its name.", "Moulder says overnight callbacks no longer pile up the same way, and she can set her phone down knowing Jenny has the line."],
  tools: ["ServiceTitan AI Virtual Agent (\"Jenny\")", "ServiceTitan scheduling and CRM"],
  cost: null,
  setupTime: null,
  start: ["List what happens to calls after you close and on weekends: voicemail, answering service, or nothing.", "If you already use field-service software, ask whether it has a built-in AI phone agent that can book into your real schedule.", "Decide which job types the AI can book (including same-night emergencies) and when it must transfer to a person.", "If you serve Spanish-speaking customers, confirm the agent handles both languages before you go live.", "Listen to the first week of recordings and fix job types or escalation rules that send callers to the wrong next step."],
