  title: "A lawn care owner uses ChatGPT to price aeration from his CRM data and write the texts his techs send",
  business: "Shades of Green (Lafayette, Indiana; owner Cory Overman)", type: "trades",
  job: "Works out the average cost per single and double pass and the seed needed per square foot from the company's CRM production data, and drafts the pre-written texts technicians send customers when they spot a lawn problem, plus service descriptions, job descriptions and complex Excel functions.",
  story: "Cory Overman started Shades of Green, a lawn care company in Lafayette, Indiana, in 2009 while finishing college. A complaint years ago from a customer whose fine fescue lawn went dormant in summer, something nobody had explained to her, pushed him to build better customer communication. The company now uses a texting platform connected to its CRM and scheduling software: Overman sets up filters called condition codes, and when a technician spots something like dollar spot, they can send a pre-written text that ChatGPT helps write, as a way to passively upsell services without being pushy. \"Pretty much anything we send out via text we run through ChatGPT first,\" he told Lawn & Landscape. When he was working out what to charge for aeration and overseeding, he uploaded production data from the CRM into ChatGPT.",
  results: ["\"It would have taken us several days and many manhours to do this manually and ChatGPT figured it out in 10 minutes,\" Overman says of the aeration and overseeding cost analysis (self-reported to Lawn & Landscape).", "The company used ChatGPT to revamp its customer communications and service descriptions.", "\"People respond better to texting,\" Overman says; the article gives no reply-rate or sales figures."],
